 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds socket sessions and pushes each committed revision as it lands. Subsecond updates are practical with this route; the 15-second freshness goal below is the floor, not the target.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Self-hosting the sockets means this project owns connection capacity and reconnection behavior rather than a realtime vendor. Three obligations follow and must be designed together: the change store keeps replayable revision history and publishes reliably (a revision must not be lost between commit and socket delivery); delivery manages socket sessions and their lifecycle; browsers reconnect after drops and catch up on revisions missed while disconnected.
 
